{
  "rendered": "To calculate the diffusion time, we use the equation: t = l^2 / D\n\nWe are given the diffusion length (l) as 10 nm and the diffusion coefficient (D) for ZrO2 as 1.7 × 10^− 18 cm^2/s.\n\nFirst, we need to convert the diffusion length from nm to cm. \n1 nm = 10^− 7 cm\n10 nm = 10^− 5 cm\n\nNow we can plug in the values:\nt = (10^− 5 cm)^2 / (1.7 × 10^− 18 cm^2/s)\nt = 10^− 10 cm^2 / (1.7 × 10^− 18 cm^2/s)\nt = 5.88 × 10^8 s\n\nThere are 60 seconds in a minute, 60 minutes in an hour, and 24 hours in a day. \nt = 5.88 × 10^8 s * (1 min / 60 s) * (1 h / 60 min) * (1 day / 24 h)\nt = 5.88 × 10^8 s * (1/3600) * (1/24)\nt = 6.55 × 10^4 days\nt = 6.55 × 10^4 / 365.25 (accounting for leap years)\nt ≈ 179.3 years\n\nSo, it would take a lithium ion approximately 179.3 years to cross a 10 nm ZrO2 coating.",
